ication uses a rejection-sampling rule that compares the target model\u2019s distribution with the draft model\u2019s distribution at each position. If the target model assigns enough probability to a draft token, it is accepted. If not, the token is rejected and a new token is sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s probabilities. The process stops at the first rejected token, then continues from the accepted prefix. Because the correction step exactly accounts for the difference between draft and target distributions, the final token distribution matches what the target model would produce in ordinary autoregressive decoding, assuming the same random seed and sampling parameters.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how often the target model accepts draft tokens, the cost of running the target model on a longer sequence, and the relative cost of the draft model. If the draft model is well aligned with the target and the task is predictable, many tokens are accepted and the target\u2019s parallel verification pays off. If the draft model is weak, the task is highly stochastic, or the target model\u2019s batch/sequence overhead is large, fewer tokens are accepted and the extra computation can erase the gain.\n\nThe two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are low draft-target agreement, causing frequent rejections, and high verification overhead, where the target model\u2019s cost to check the proposed sequence is too large relative to the number of accepted tokens.\n\nCount words:\nSpeculative1 decoding2 speeds3 up", "top2": [[[1596, -0.0018879104172810912], [22916, -6.751292705535889]], [[1144, -9.775209946383256e-06], [4087, -12.448492050170898]], [[4087, -0.23520328104496002], [310, -1.5723137855529785]], [[1156, -0.00198358204215765], [303, -6.305606842041016]], [[579, -0.23050063848495483], [25, -1.771888256072998]], [[1622, -0.008757260628044605], [3296, -4.962028980255127]], [[25, -0.2819195091724396], [13, -